  "success": true,
  "output": "## Where things stand\n\nMost of this repo is pre-implementation — `engine/pipeline/*.py`, `engine/eval/*.py`, and `engine/db.py` are `NotImplementedError` stubs (Phase 2+ per `AGENTS.md`). There's no live query path, no rendered conflict queue, and no resolution agent yet, so most of the categories you asked about (N+1 queries in the pipeline, memory leaks in a long-running match loop, redundant embedding calls) don't exist as *code* yet — only as *risk* that the stubs need to guard against when filled in. I'll flag both: real issues in what's implemented (web layer), and specific landmines in the stubs worth avoiding on first implementation.\n\n## 1. Real issue today: redundant query instead of caching (`web/lib/server/sources.ts`)\n\n```ts\nexport async function listSources(): Promise<Source[]> {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n}\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // full row fetch + Zod-parse, just to count\n  return { crm: ..., warehouse: ..., synthetic: ... };\n}\n```\n\n`countSourcesBySide` (used by `get-started/page.tsx`) pulls every row and every `config` JSONB blob and runs them through `sourceSchema.parse` just to produce three integers. Cheap today at demo scale, but it's the wrong shape — and once `sources/page.tsx` and `get-started/page.tsx` are both reachable in the same nav flow, you'll get two independent round trips for overlapping data with no dedup.\n\nFix: push the count into SQL, and dedupe repeated calls within a request with React's `cache()`.\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, n: sql<number>`count(*)` })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\nWrapping `listSources` in `cache()` also means if a future page calls it twice in one render (e.g. header count + table), it's one DB hit, not two — the actual N+1 risk once more pages land.\n\n## 2. React re-renders — nothing concerning yet\n\nThe only client components are `NavRail` (`usePathname` + Clerk's `useUser`) and `SignOutControl`. Both are small, leaf-level, and correctly marked `\"use client\"` at the leaf per `AGENTS.md`'s rule — no prop-drilling, no derived state causing cascades. Nothing to fix here. The real re-render risk arrives with the **conflict queue** (Phase 4, not built): a 400-row keyboard-navigable list (`j`/`k`/`a`/`e`/`o`) is exactly the shape that causes whole-list re-renders if selection state lives above the row instead of in a row-keyed lookup. Worth pre-committing to `React.memo` on `ConflictDiffRow` and keeping \"which row is focused\" in a ref/atom rather than in a state var that the parent re-renders on every keypress, when you build it.\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities that are already mandated but not yet wired\n\n`AGENTS.md` itself specifies two caching requirements that aren't implemented yet:\n\n- **Prompt caching on the ruleset block** in `agent/graph.py`/`agent/prompts.py` — the ruleset is identical across every conflict in a run, called out as \"the single largest cost lever.\" When you implement `propose`, cache-control the ruleset block, not the per-conflict diff.\n- **Batch API for `eval/harness.py` sweeps** — `sweep_auto_apply_threshold` will call the resolution agent repeatedly across a threshold grid; since eval runs aren't latency-sensitive, that's a Batch API candidate at half cost, not sequential/synchronous calls.\n\nNeither is a bug today (the functions raise `NotImplementedError`), but they're worth building in from the first cut rather than retrofitting.\n\n## 4. The N+1 / O(n²) risk that matters most: `blocking.py`\n\n```python\ndef block(side_a, side_b, *, top_k: int = TOP_K) -> list[tuple[...]]:\n    raise NotImplementedError(...)\n```\n\nThe docstring already states the constraint correctly: *\"Blocking exists so the pipeline is O(n·k), not O(n²) — NEVER compare all pairs.\"* The concrete failure mode to avoid when this gets implemented: calling `sentence-transformers` encode() **per entity** inside a loop (an N+1-shaped pattern against the embedding model) instead of batching:\n\n```python\n# Avoid — one model call per entity\nembeddings = [model.encode(e.normalizedName) for e in side_a]\n\n# Batch instead — one call for the whole side\nembeddings = model.encode([e.normalizedName for e in side_a], batch_size=64)\n```\n\nSame caution applies to FAISS index construction: build the index once per run, not once per query.\n\n## 5. Memory leaks — none observed; one thing to watch\n\n`web/lib/db/index.ts` correctly caches the postgres client on `globalThis` to survive Next.js HMR reloads, which is the standard fix for the \"new pool per hot-reload\" leak — good as-is.\n\nThe one place to watch going forward is `engine/main.py`'s `BackgroundTasks` use for `execute_run`: FastAPI `BackgroundTasks` run in-process with no queue, no retry, and no bound on concurrent runs. If two runs are kicked off close together, both hold their full `entities`/`matches` working set in memory simultaneously with nothing releasing it until the background task returns. Once `execute_run` is implemented, make sure large intermediate structures (embeddings, candidate pairs) are dropped/scoped per stage rather than accumulated in one long-lived function frame across all five pipeline stages.\n\n## 6. Redundant computation to pre-empt: `normalize.py`\n\n`fold_name` is described as \"deterministic and total\" and will be called for every entity, on every run, and again inside blocking/matching for comparison. If the same source names recur across a run (parent/child chains, repeated territory strings), an unbounded `functools.lru_cache` on `fold_name` avoids redoing the same string-folding work:\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef fold_name(name: str) -> str:\n    ...\n```\n\nSafe because it's pure and total — no cache invalidation concern.\n\n---\n\n**Net**: no live N+1 queries, no leak, no wasted re-render exists in the shipped code today because there isn't much shipped code yet. The one concrete fix worth making now is #1 (`countSourcesBySide`). The rest are guardrails to build into the Phase 2/3 stubs on first implementation rather than issues to refactor later — cheaper to get right once than to profile and fix after `eval/harness.py` is measuring real numbers.\n",
